# dbt Architecture Rules

## Layer Responsibilities

### Staging (`stg_*`)
- 1:1 with source tables. One staging model per source table.
- Rename columns to snake_case. Cast types. No joins. No aggregations.
- Light cleaning only: trim whitespace, handle obvious nulls, parse dates.
- References `{{ source('raw_schema', 'table') }}` — the ONLY layer that uses `{{ source() }}`.
- Materialized as `view` (cheap, always fresh).

### Intermediate (`int_*`) — optional
- Complex multi-source joins, pivots, business logic that is reused by multiple marts.
- Not exposed to BI tools. Use `ephemeral` or `view` materialization.
- Use when the same join/logic appears in more than one mart.

### Marts (`fct_*`, `dim_*`)
- Business-ready. Aggregated facts, conformed dimensions. Exposed to BI tools.
- Fact tables (`fct_*`): measures at a defined grain (e.g., one row per order per day).
- Dimension tables (`dim_*`): descriptive attributes (e.g., customers, products).
- Materialized as `table` (fast for BI) or `incremental` (large tables).

## Source and Ref Discipline
- `{{ source() }}` in staging only: `{{ source('raw_ecommerce', 'orders') }}`.
- `{{ ref() }}` everywhere else: `{{ ref('stg_orders') }}`, `{{ ref('dim_customers') }}`.
- Never hardcode `database.schema.table` in any model. dbt resolves the correct target schema from the profile.
- Enforced by dbt-checkpoint pre-commit hook: `check-source-refs`.

## Incremental Models
- Use `{{ config(materialized='incremental') }}` for large fact tables.
- Always include `is_incremental()` guard to filter only new/updated rows:
  ```sql
  {% if is_incremental() %}
  WHERE updated_at > (SELECT MAX(updated_at) FROM {{ this }})
  {% endif %}
  ```
- Define `unique_key` in config to handle late-arriving or duplicate data:
  ```sql
  {{ config(materialized='incremental', unique_key='order_id') }}
  ```
- Start with `table` materialization; switch to `incremental` only when build time becomes a problem.

## Materializations Reference
| Materialization | Use case | When to use |
|---|---|---|
| `view` | Staging models | Always fresh, no storage cost |
| `table` | Marts | Fast BI queries, full refresh each run |
| `incremental` | Large fact tables | Daily growth, slow full refresh |
| `ephemeral` | Intermediate CTEs | No table needed, embedded in downstream |

## Macros for Reuse
- Extract repeated SQL patterns into `macros/`. Use `{{ macro_name(args) }}` in models.
- Do not copy-paste SQL across models. If you copy SQL, extract a macro.
- Common macros: `cents_to_dollars(column)`, `safe_divide(numerator, denominator)`, `generate_surrogate_key(fields)`.
- Prefer `dbt_utils` built-in macros before writing custom ones.

## Packages
- Use `dbt_utils` for: `generate_surrogate_key`, `pivot`, `date_spine`, `get_column_values`.
- Use `dbt-expectations` for: range tests, regex tests, cardinality checks.
- Use `dbt-checkpoint` for: pre-commit hooks enforcing source/ref discipline.
- Pin all package versions in `packages.yml`. Run `dbt deps` after updating.
- Review package changelogs before upgrading — breaking changes in dbt_utils are common across minor versions.
